  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the cross-repo trace found:\n\n**FastAPI side \u2014 body \u2192 validation invocation:**\n- `fastapi/dependencies/utils.py`: `get_dependant`/`analyze_param` build a `ModelField` for the body parameter; `solve_dependencies` calls `request_body_to_args(required_params=dependant.body_params, received_body=body)`; that function calls `field.validate(value, ...)` per body field before the route handler ever runs. `get_body_field` builds the synthetic `Body_*` model when multiple body params exist.\n- `fastapi/_compat.py`: the Pydantic-v2 branch's `ModelField.validate()` \u2014 this is the actual FastAPI\u2192Pydantic boundary. It wraps a `TypeAdapter(Annotated[annotation, field_info])` built in `__post_init__` and calls `self._type_adapter.validate_python(value, from_attributes=True)`. Note: FastAPI validates via `validate_python` on an already-decoded dict, not `model_validate_json` directly.\n- `fastapi/routing.py`: `get_request_handler`'s inner `app()` reads `await request.body()`, decodes it via `await request.json()` into a plain dict/list (`json_body`), and passes that decoded value into `solve_dependencies(body=...)` \u2014 this is where raw JSON becomes the Python object handed to Pydantic.\n\n**Pydantic v2 side \u2014 discriminated union + model_validator wiring:**\n- `pydantic/_internal/_generate_schema.py`: `apply_model_validators()` wraps the generated model schema in `function-before`/`function-after`/`function-wrap` core-schema nodes per validator mode; discriminator application (`_discriminated_union.apply_discriminators(schema)`) runs over the *already-built* core schema \u2014 after model-validator wrapping \u2014 walking down into tagged-union metadata placeholders.\n- `pydantic/_internal/_discriminated_union.py`: `apply_discriminator`/`_ApplyInferredDiscriminator` convert a `Union[...]` schema into a `tagged-union` core schema keyed by the discriminator field's `Literal` values. Discriminator resolution only ever inspects the value **present at that field's position** in the input \u2014 it has no visibility into a `mode='before'` model validator that ran earlier and can rewrite/drop that key from the incoming dict before the union field is even reached.\n- `pydantic/functional_validators.py`: `model_validator(mode='before')` decorator, `ModelBeforeValidator`/`FreeModelBeforeValidator` protocols \u2014 confirms a before-validator receives the raw input (dict/whatever was passed to `validate_python`/`validate_json`) and returns a (possibly mutated) value that flows into field-level validation, including the tagged-union lookup.\n- `pydantic/_internal/_decorators.py`: `ModelValidatorDecoratorInfo` \u2014 how `mode` is tracked and validators are collected/ordered.\n- `pydantic/main.py`: `model_validate_json` \u2014 JSON\u2192Python decode entry point (used by direct Pydantic parsing; FastAPI itself goes through `request.json()` + `validate_python` as noted above).\n- `pydantic/types.py` / `pydantic/fields.py`: `Discriminator` class and `Field(discriminator=...)` wiring that produces the `CORE_SCHEMA_METADATA_DISCRIMINATOR_PLACEHOLDER_KEY` metadata consumed by `_discriminated_union.py`.\n- `fastapi/params.py` / `fastapi/param_functions.py`: FastAPI's `Body(discriminator=...)` passthrough that ends up as the field's `Annotated[..., Field(discriminator=...)]` metadata.\n\n**Root cause of the silent field drop:** a `model_validator(mode='before')` on the request-body model runs *before* pydantic-core reaches the discriminated-union field. If that before-validator strips or renames the key holding the union payload (e.g., an allow-list filter, key-casing normalization, or a \"clean up legacy v1 payload\" shim written during the v1\u2192v2 migration), the tagged-union field simply has no input for that key. Since the field is `Optional[...] = None` (or otherwise has a default), pydantic-core doesn't raise `union_tag_not_found` \u2014 it just applies the field default. No error is raised, discriminator matching never runs, and the response serializes the field as `None`, giving the customer's exact symptom: `200 OK` with a silently-dropped field.",
